\title{nos.tlplotR: Visualize Newcastle-Ottawa Scale Risk-of-Bias Assessments with 'nos-tlplot'}
\description{
\code{nos.tlplotR} is an R interface to the Python package
\href{https://nos-tlplot.github.io}{\code{nos-tlplot}}. It creates publication-ready
traffic-light plots and thirteen specialized visualizations for
Newcastle-Ottawa Scale (NOS) risk-of-bias assessments in systematic
reviews and meta-analyses.
}
\details{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: width=180 alt="nos.tlplotR logo"}}

The package supports:
\itemize{
\item every plot at once, via \code{\link[=plot_all]{plot_all()}};
\item the NOS visualization grid, via \code{\link[=plot_nos_visualization]{plot_nos_visualization()}};
\item the traffic-light bubble plot, via \code{\link[=plot_traffic_light]{plot_traffic_light()}};
\item domain and theme radar charts, via \code{\link[=plot_domain_radar]{plot_domain_radar()}} and
\code{\link[=plot_theme_radar]{plot_theme_radar()}};
\item domain and item-level star heatmaps, via \code{\link[=plot_domain_heatmap]{plot_domain_heatmap()}} and
\code{\link[=plot_item_heatmap]{plot_item_heatmap()}};
\item stacked domain stars, via \code{\link[=plot_dot_profile]{plot_dot_profile()}};
\item the score table, via \code{\link[=plot_score_table]{plot_score_table()}};
\item the risk donut chart, via \code{\link[=plot_donut]{plot_donut()}};
\item the star ratings matrix, via \code{\link[=plot_star_matrix]{plot_star_matrix()}};
\item the total score lollipop chart, via \code{\link[=plot_lollipop]{plot_lollipop()}};
\item the overall risk pie chart, via \code{\link[=plot_risk_pie]{plot_risk_pie()}};
\item the stacked area risk chart, via \code{\link[=plot_stacked_area]{plot_stacked_area()}}.
}
\subsection{Python requirement}{

A Python installation (>= 3.12) with the \code{nos-tlplot} package is required.
On first load, \code{nos.tlplotR} declares \code{nos-tlplot} as a Python requirement
with \code{\link[reticulate:py_require]{reticulate::py_require()}}, so \code{reticulate} can provide it
automatically. To use an existing Python installation instead, install the
module yourself:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{reticulate::py_install("nos-tlplot", pip = TRUE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Use \code{\link[reticulate:py_config]{reticulate::py_config()}} to check which Python interpreter is in use
and \code{\link[reticulate:py_module_available]{reticulate::py_module_available()}} to check that \code{nos_tlplot} is
available.
}

\subsection{Input data}{

Every plotting function accepts a data frame or a path to a CSV (\code{.csv})
